Fix an unformatted template string in Amazon's SongLink resolver.

`source_url` is a module-level constant (amazon.py:59):

    source_url = "https://open.spotify.com/track/{track_id}"

Nothing ever calls .format() on it, so resolution step 4 puts the literal
placeholder on the wire and every request 400s:

    links?url=https://open.spotify.com/track/{track_id}&userCountry=US

Observed in production 2026-08-04 on four separate tracks; the route has
never succeeded for any track. `track_id` is in scope here — the same
function's failure message at the end of the chain prints the real value.

Not a missing f-prefix: the string is defined at module level where
`track_id` does not exist, so an f-string there would raise NameError at
import. The template is deliberate; only the substitution is missing.

Worth knowing: the ISRC variant just below (step 5) is correctly formatted
and still returns 400, so song.link may be refusing us regardless. This
fixes the bug either way — a route that cannot possibly work is worse than
one that merely might not.

diff --git a/SpotiFLAC/providers/amazon.py b/SpotiFLAC/providers/amazon.py
--- a/SpotiFLAC/providers/amazon.py
+++ b/SpotiFLAC/providers/amazon.py
@@ -476,7 +476,8 @@

         # 4. SONGLINK API (Spotify ID)
         try:
-            sl_api_url = f"https://api.song.link/v1-alpha.1/links?url={source_url}&userCountry=US"
+            spotify_url = source_url.format(track_id=track_id)
+            sl_api_url = f"https://api.song.link/v1-alpha.1/links?url={spotify_url}&userCountry=US"
             resp = await self._async_http.get(sl_api_url, timeout=15)
             data = resp.json()
             links = data.get("linksByPlatform", {})
